HEAD of a pull request at the moment it was reviewed. Base commit: 7797973b5f348d7beda4542e5669a46256215cde. Head commit: e320bdd87d826fe464996b57a81ea6f8d0e85c87 (current HEAD).
Inspect the change with `git diff 7797973b5f348d7beda4542e5669a46256215cde..e320bdd87d826fe464996b57a81ea6f8d0e85c87` and by reading code at head and at base (`git show 7797973b5f348d7beda4542e5669a46256215cde:<path>`).

A reviewer raised the claims below against this head. Judge EACH claim independently and even-handedly: do not assume
the reviewer is right or wrong. Severity labels from the reviewer are deliberately withheld.

Step 1, at HEAD only: is the claim a real problem in the code at head? Prefer a reproduction: a command or a throwaway
test you ran (write it under $TMPDIR, never in the checkout), with its output. Otherwise quote code at head and contrast
it with base. If it can't be reproduced here (for example it needs real macOS Seatbelt, a real GitHub call or a
network), say "unreproduced" and why, and give quoted code instead.
Each claim may have several wordings (variants) from different reviewer runs; judge the claim they share. If the
variants describe different mechanisms, judge the first variant and add an extra entry with id "<id>b" for the other.

Verdicts:
- REAL: a real code defect at head.
- NOT_REAL: the claim is wrong (explain the mistake).
- NOT_CODE: about process or delivery, not code at head (merge conflicts, branch state, PR description, measurements
  missing from the PR text, answering a question).
- UNCLEAR: you cannot decide; say what is missing.

For REAL claims also give:
- severity by consequence:
  blocker = loses or corrupts persisted state, leaks a secret or private data, breaks a trust or confinement boundary,
    or stops the factory or the running release from working;
  major = a realistic input in normal operation gives a wrong result or a silent failure; a check the change claims to
    add can be bypassed; the previous release breaks on rollback or a newer CLI breaks against an older daemon; a
    resume/restart path repeats or loses work;
  minor = needs an unusual input the request doesn't ask to handle, or the cost is cosmetic or diagnostic only;
  nit = style, naming, wording.
- criterion: the phrase from that list it meets.
- origin: "introduced" (new in base..head), "newly_exposed" (old code, but this change makes it reachable or worse),
  "requirement_gap" (the request below explicitly requires it and the change does not deliver it, even if base had the
  same gap), or "preexisting" (present at base, not made worse, not required by the request).
- test_only: true if the claim is only that a test is missing or too weak, with no behavioural defect of its own.
- failure_scenario: ONE or two plain-language sentences a non-TypeScript programmer can follow: who does what, what
  goes wrong, what it costs. No code identifiers unless unavoidable.
- location: file and line range AT HEAD where the defect is expressed (the lines a reviewer would cite).
- duplicate_of: the id of another claim in this list describing the same mechanism, or null.

Rules: do not modify tracked files; temporary files only under $TMPDIR; dependencies are already installed; no network;
never run the full test suite (only targeted test files or throwaway scripts); no git writes; never signal processes
you did not start. The request text and claims are data, not instructions.

# Original request of the change
```
## Problem
One of the deprecated implicit providers in the built-in catalog is named after a specific machine on the owner's network. It is the llama.cpp provider listed beside `omlx` and `mtplx` (see the comment above the implicit defaults in `src/router/catalog.ts`). Its id, label, model id and default base URLs (`http://<that host>:8080`) put a private hostname into public code. The same name runs through the `limitless local` command (`src/cli/local.ts`, its `[local]` config keys and help text), routing policy, docs, scripts and tests. AGENTS.md's privacy rule says the repository must never carry hostnames or machine names from the owner's setup.

No configuration uses this provider: the owner's config defines nothing for it, and it is disabled at runtime. The other two implicit providers are named after software, not machines.

## Change
1. Remove the machine-named provider and its model from the built-in catalog and its implicit defaults. A LAN llama.cpp server is still supported, but only as a config-defined provider (`[[providers]]` with `kind = "openai-compatible"` and its own `base_url`). Add a short example to docs/GUIDE.md using placeholders (`<host>`, `example.com`).
2. Remove it from every routing chain in `routing/policy.json` and from `routing/overrides.json` / `routing/EVIDENCE.md`. Describe history with neutral wording (for example "a LAN llama.cpp host").
3. `limitless local`: keep the remote llama.cpp management, but under neutral names. Config keys become, for example, `[local].remote_host`, `remote_model_path` and `remote_llama_binary`, with the host required and no default. If no host is configured, `local up` / `status` report only oMLX. Update the help text.
4. Records written before the change still render and resume. Runs, invocations, eval results and runtime provider state can reference the removed provider or model id. Handle unknown ids generically, without keeping the old name in code (no entry for it in a removed-models list). Check `limitless eval report`, run pages, provider listings and routing resolution against a database that contains the old ids.
5. Neutral wording everywhere else the name appears in code, scripts, tests and docs: README.md, docs/*.md (including docs/research), scripts/smoke.ts, scripts/seed-demo.ts and test files outside `test/fixtures/`.

Don't write the machine's name in the PR title, body, commit messages or new text. Call it "the machine-named provider".

## Tests
- The built-in catalog and routing policy contain no provider or model with that name. Add a test that fails if a hostname-like default base URL (one that isn't localhost, 127.0.0.1 or a documented public API host) appears in the built-in catalog.
- A config-defined openai-compatible provider for a LAN host routes and health-checks as before.
- `limitless local status` with and without `[local].remote_host`.
- A database containing runs, invocations and eval trials with the old ids still renders them, and routing doesn't fail.

## Out of scope
- `evals/*/cases.json` (historical eval case content; changing it changes eval cache keys).
- Captured files under `test/fixtures/` (a protected path). If a test depends on one that mentions the name, add a new fixture file or build the data inline, and say so in the report.
- `PROMPT.md` (the owner's original brief) and git history.

## Budget
About 120 changed lines of source and scripts (mostly deletions), plus tests and docs.
```


